---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { frontendSeries } from '../../data/series';
const inlineStyle = `
/* ── Hero ── */
.fe13-hero{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#00b894 0%,#0984e3 50%,#6c5ce7 100%)}

/* ── compilation arrows animation ── */
.compile-art{margin:2.5rem auto 0;max-width:18rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0.5rem}
.compile-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem;opacity:0;animation:compileIn 0.5s ease forwards}
.compile-row:nth-child(1){animation-delay:0s}
.compile-row:nth-child(2){animation-delay:0.3s}
.compile-row:nth-child(3){animation-delay:0.6s}
.compile-from{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.65rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.25rem 0.6rem;border-radius:0.375rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.15);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);min-width:4rem;text-align:center}
.compile-arrow-txt{color:rgba(255,255,255,0.5);font-size:0.8rem}
.compile-to{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.65rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.25rem 0.6rem;border-radius:0.375rem;background:rgba(9,132,227,0.3);color:rgba(150,220,255,0.9)}
@keyframes compileIn{from{opacity:0;transform:translateX(-8px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}

.fe13-num{color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}

/* ── Quiz theming ep13 ── */
.quiz-ep13f .quiz-q-num{color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}
.quiz-ep13f .quiz-reveal-btn{color:#00b894}
.quiz-ep13f .quiz-opt-letter{color:#00b894}
.quiz-ep13f .quiz-option:hover{border-color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}
.quiz-ep13f .quiz-answer-highlight{background:#e8f8f5;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894}
`;
const bodyHtml = `

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero fe13-hero">
  <div class="hero-badge">Episode 13 of 23</div>
  <h1>TypeScript, JSX<br>&amp; Vue Templates</h1>
  <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.95)">Three frameworks. Three different ways to write code. None of them run in a browser. All of them compile to one thing.</p>
  <div class="compile-art">
    <div class="compile-row">
      <span class="compile-from">TypeScript</span>
      <span class="compile-arrow-txt">→</span>
      <span class="compile-to">plain JavaScript</span>
    </div>
    <div class="compile-row">
      <span class="compile-from">JSX</span>
      <span class="compile-arrow-txt">→</span>
      <span class="compile-to">plain JavaScript</span>
    </div>
    <div class="compile-row">
      <span class="compile-from">Vue template</span>
      <span class="compile-arrow-txt">→</span>
      <span class="compile-to">plain JavaScript</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe13-num">01</span>
  <h2>The Fundamental Truth</h2>
  <p>Browsers understand three things: <span class="highlight">HTML</span>, <span class="highlight">CSS</span>, and <span class="highlight">plain JavaScript</span>. That is the complete list. Nothing else.</p>
  <p>TypeScript is not plain JavaScript. JSX is not plain JavaScript. A .vue file with its template/script/style sections is definitely not plain JavaScript.</p>
  <p>So how does any of this work? Every one of these formats must be <span class="highlight-orange">compiled</span> — transformed into plain JavaScript before it reaches the browser. The browser never sees TypeScript, JSX, or .vue files. It only sees the compiled output.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: TypeScript -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe13-num">02</span>
  <h2>TypeScript: JavaScript with Types</h2>
  <p>TypeScript was created by <span class="highlight">Microsoft</span>. It is a superset of JavaScript — meaning all valid JavaScript is also valid TypeScript. TypeScript adds one thing: <span class="highlight-orange">type annotations</span>.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">// Plain JavaScript — no types</span>
<span class="code-keyword">function</span> add(a, b) {
  <span class="code-keyword">return</span> a + b;
}
add(<span class="code-string">"5"</span>, 10);  <span class="code-comment">// → "510" (string concat, not addition!)</span>
<span class="code-comment">// JavaScript won't warn you — it runs silently wrong</span>

<span class="code-comment">// TypeScript — with types</span>
<span class="code-keyword">function</span> add(a: <span class="code-keyword">number</span>, b: <span class="code-keyword">number</span>): <span class="code-keyword">number</span> {
  <span class="code-keyword">return</span> a + b;
}
add(<span class="code-string">"5"</span>, 10);
<span class="code-comment">// Error: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'</span>
<span class="code-comment">// TypeScript catches this before you ever run the code</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>The <code style="font-size:0.875rem">: number</code> annotations are TypeScript — they tell the compiler what types are expected. When you compile TypeScript, <span class="highlight">all type annotations are stripped out</span>. What remains is identical to plain JavaScript, which is exactly what the browser needs.</p>
  <p>Angular requires TypeScript. Vue 3 is written in TypeScript. This repo uses TypeScript for its .ts files.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: JSX -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe13-num">03</span>
  <h2>JSX: HTML-Looking Syntax Inside JavaScript</h2>
  <p>JSX is primarily used in React. It lets you write what looks like HTML directly in JavaScript code:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">// JSX (what you write)</span>
<span class="code-keyword">function</span> Button({ label }) {
  <span class="code-keyword">return</span> (
    <span class="code-string">&lt;button className="btn" onClick={handleClick}&gt;</span>
      <span class="code-string">{label}</span>
    <span class="code-string">&lt;/button&gt;</span>
  );
}

<span class="code-comment">// Compiled to plain JavaScript (what the browser sees)</span>
<span class="code-keyword">function</span> Button({ label }) {
  <span class="code-keyword">return</span> React.createElement(
    <span class="code-string">'button'</span>,
    { className: <span class="code-string">'btn'</span>, onClick: handleClick },
    label
  );
}</pre>
  </div>

  <p>The browser cannot run JSX — there are no angle-bracket-in-JavaScript syntax rules. The JSX compiler (usually Babel or the TypeScript compiler) converts every JSX expression into a <code style="font-size:0.875rem">React.createElement()</code> call. The browser receives and runs plain JavaScript function calls.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: Vue Templates -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe13-num">04</span>
  <h2>Vue Templates: HTML with Superpowers</h2>
  <p>Vue's template syntax looks almost like regular HTML, but with special directives:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">&lt;!-- Vue template (what you write) --&gt;</span>
<span class="code-keyword">&lt;template&gt;</span>
  <span class="code-keyword">&lt;button</span>
    @click="handleClick"
    :class="{ active: isActive }"
  <span class="code-keyword">&gt;</span>
    {{ label }}
  <span class="code-keyword">&lt;/button&gt;</span>
<span class="code-keyword">&lt;/template&gt;</span>

<span class="code-comment">// Vue compiler output (simplified)</span>
<span class="code-keyword">function</span> render() {
  <span class="code-keyword">return</span> createElement(<span class="code-string">'button'</span>, {
    on: { click: handleClick },
    class: { active: <span class="code-keyword">this</span>.isActive }
  }, <span class="code-keyword">this</span>.label);
}</pre>
  </div>

  <p>The <code style="font-size:0.875rem">@click</code>, <code style="font-size:0.875rem">:class</code>, and <code style="font-size:0.875rem">{{ }}</code> syntax is Vue-specific — no browser understands it. The Vue compiler converts the template into a plain JavaScript render function. The browser receives and runs that function.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: the table -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe13-num">05</span>
  <h2>The Full Picture</h2>
  <p>This is about how you <span class="highlight">write</span> code — not what it ultimately runs as:</p>

  <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:1.5rem">
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.875rem">
      <thead>
        <tr style="background:#f0efff">
          <th style="padding:0.75rem 1rem;text-align:left;border-bottom:0.125rem solid #e0e0e0">Framework</th>
          <th style="padding:0.75rem 1rem;text-align:left;border-bottom:0.125rem solid #e0e0e0">How you write</th>
          <th style="padding:0.75rem 1rem;text-align:left;border-bottom:0.125rem solid #e0e0e0">Browser runs</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:0.0625rem solid #f0f0f0">
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">jQuery (old)</td>
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">Plain JavaScript</td>
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">Same plain JavaScript</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:0.0625rem solid #f0f0f0;background:#fafafa">
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">Angular</td>
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">TypeScript + templates</td>
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">Compiled plain JavaScript</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:0.0625rem solid #f0f0f0">
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">React</td>
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">JSX (looks like HTML in JS)</td>
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">React.createElement() calls</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="background:#fafafa">
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">Vue</td>
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">.vue template syntax</td>
          <td style="padding:0.75rem 1rem">Compiled render function</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">The key insight: all of this is about how you <span class="highlight">author</span> code. Everything ends up as plain JavaScript before it reaches the browser. The browser has no idea whether the original source was TypeScript, JSX, or Vue. It just runs JavaScript.</p>
</section>

<!-- RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>
  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">TypeScript</strong> = JavaScript + type annotations. Types are stripped at compile time. Browser runs plain JavaScript.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">JSX</strong> = HTML-looking syntax in JavaScript. Compiled to React.createElement() calls. Browser runs plain JavaScript.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">Vue templates</strong> = HTML with special directives. Compiled to render functions. Browser runs plain JavaScript.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">The browser</strong> only understands HTML, CSS, and plain JavaScript. Everything else must be compiled down.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKPOINT QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep13f">
  <span class="section-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">When TypeScript is compiled, what happens to the type annotations?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>They are converted to JavaScript comments</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>They are kept in the output and run by the browser</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>They are completely stripped out — only plain JavaScript remains</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>They are converted to runtime checks (if/else statements)</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) They are completely stripped out — only plain JavaScript remains</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">TypeScript type annotations (: number, : string, interface, etc.) exist only during development — they help your editor catch bugs. When compiled, they are entirely removed. The output is plain JavaScript with no trace of types. The browser only sees and runs JavaScript.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">JSX syntax like &lt;button onClick={fn}&gt;Label&lt;/button&gt; gets compiled to what?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>An HTML string that is injected into innerHTML</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>A React.createElement() function call</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>A CSS-in-JS style object</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>A DOM API call like document.createElement()</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) A React.createElement() function call</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Every JSX element is syntactic sugar for React.createElement(tag, props, children). The JSX compiler transforms the visual HTML-like syntax into plain JavaScript function calls. The browser receives and runs those function calls — it never sees the JSX.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What are the three things a browser can natively understand and run?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>HTML, TypeScript, and CSS</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>JSX, CSS, and JSON</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>HTML, CSS, and plain JavaScript</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Vue templates, CSS, and JavaScript</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) HTML, CSS, and plain JavaScript</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Browsers natively understand HTML (structure), CSS (styling), and plain JavaScript (behavior). TypeScript, JSX, Vue templates, Sass, and every other format must be compiled or transpiled into one of these three before the browser can use them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- NEXT -->
<section class="next-part" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#e17055,#0984e3)">
  <h2>Ready for Episode 14?</h2>
  <p>TypeScript needs to compile. JSX needs to compile. Vue templates need to compile. Sass needs to compile. And you want everything minified and bundled. All of this is what build tools do — and why they exist.</p>
  <a href="part14.html" class="btn btn-white">Continue to Episode 14 &#10132;</a>
</section>
`;
---
<ArticleLayout title="How the Frontend Works — Part 13: TypeScript, JSX & Vue Templates | Unbox the Black Box" inlineStyle={inlineStyle} series={frontendSeries} episodeNum={13} hasQuiz={true}>
<Fragment set:html={bodyHtml} />
</ArticleLayout>
